STM32L432 Advanced PC Fan Speed Controller with Overspeed Alarm
Author: Martins Osemudiamen Okoh
TU Dublin ID: A0004717
Course: Embedded Systems Project – EENG1030

Project Description
This embedded system project implements a real-time, closed-loop motor speed monitoring and control system using an STM32L432KC microcontroller. The system reads rotational speed from a quadrature encoder for user input and a tachometer for fan feedback. It controls the fan speed via PWM output, provides visual feedback using an LED, and activates an audible buzzer alarm when the fan exceeds a safe speed threshold of 3500 RPM. A serial debug interface and an SPI display provide real-time status information.

The video showcase of this project can be viewed via: https://youtu.be/aQIbt7y0xQ4?si=APt8LlkEA8uwMtla

Aim & Objectives
Aim: Design and implementation of an advanced PC fan speed controller using an STM32L432KC microcontroller with an overspeed alarm.

Objectives:

1) Schematic design of the PC fan speed controller.
2) Software implementation.
3) Hardware implementation.
4) Testing / Debugging.
5) Documentation.

Hardware Requirements
Components List
1) STM32L432KC microcontroller board (e.g., Nucleo-L432KC)
2) 12V PC Fan with 4-wire connection (PWM control & Tachometer output)
3) Quadrature rotary encoder
4) 1.8" TFT SPI LCD (ST7735S)
5) LED (visual status indicator)
6) Buzzer (audible alarm)
7) Push button (for tachometer input)
8) 330 Ohm resistor
9) Breadboard and connecting wires
10) 12V DC Bench Power Supply
11) USB-to-UART converter / USB Cable (for serial debugging)

System Pin Connections
    Component	        Signal	              STM32L432 Pin	Function
1)  Rotary Encoder      CLK	                  PA0	TIM2_CH1 (Encoder Input)
                        DT	                  PB3	TIM2_CH2 (Encoder Input)
                        VCC                   3.3V	Power
                        GND	                  GND	Ground
    
2)  PC Fan	            PWM (Blue)	          PA6	TIM16_CH1 (Speed Control)
                        Tachometer (Green)	  PB4	EXTI4 (Speed Feedback)
                        GND (Black)	          GND	Ground
                        +12V (Yellow)	        12V Supply	Power
    
3)  LED Indicator	    Anode	                PB5	Overspeed Status
                        Cathode	              GND (via 330Ω)	
    
4)  Buzzer	            Positive	            PB6	Audible Alarm
                        Negative	            GND	
    
5)  LCD Screen (SPI)    LCD	                  3.3v
                        SCL	                  PA5	SPI1_SCK
                        SDA	                  PA7	SPI1_MOSI
                        CS	                  PA4	Chip Select
                        RESET	                PB0	Reset
                        DC	                  PB1	Data/Command
                        VCC	                  3.3V	Power
                        GND	                  GND	Ground
    
6)  Serial Debug	    TX	                  PA2	USART2_TX
                        RX	                  PA15	USART2_RX

Features
1) Encoder-based Speed Control: A quadrature encoder connected to TIM2 (Mode 3) provides precise user input for setting the fan speed.
2) PWM Generation: Timer 16 produces a 25 kHz PWM signal on PA6, with a duty cycle adjustable from 0% to 100% based on the encoder position.
3) Tachometer Speed Calculation: Measures actual fan RPM by counting tachometer pulses (2 pulses/rev) on PB4 over a 0.1333-second interval.
4) Overspeed Detection & Alarm: Triggers an audiovisual alarm when the RPM exceeds 3500.
5) Visual Alarm: LED (PB5) turns OFF (normally ON when speed is safe).
6) Audible Alarm: Buzzer (PB6) generates a 500 Hz square wave.
7) Real-Time Monitoring: Outputs encoder value, PWM duty, and RPM to a serial console for debugging.
8) SPI Display: Shows current encoder position and fan RPM on a 1.8" TFT LCD.

        Key System Parameters
    Parameter	                      Value
1)  System Clock	                  80 MHz
2)  PWM Frequency	                  25 kHz
3)  PWM Resolution	                3200 steps (ARR = 3199)
4)  Encoder Count Range	            0 - 159 (ARR = 159)
5)  RPM Safe Threshold	            3500 RPM
6)  Buzzer Tone Frequency	          500 Hz
7)  Buzzer Timer Interrupt Rate	    1000 Hz (1 ms)
8)  Main Loop Update Rate	          10 Hz (100 ms delay)

Register Configuration Summary
Timer 2 (Encoder)
1) Mode: Quadrature encoder mode 3 (counts on both edges of TI1 and TI2)
2) Auto-reload (ARR): 159 (0-159 count range)
3) Inputs: PA0 (TI1) and PB3 (TI2) as capture inputs (Alternate Function 1)

Timer 16 (PWM for Fan Speed)
1) Prescaler (PSC): 0
2) Auto-reload (ARR): 3199 (results in 80 MHz / 3200 = 25 kHz)
3) Pulse (CCR1): 1600 (default 50% duty cycle)
4) Output: PA6 (Alternate Function 14)
5) Break and Dead-Time (BDTR): MOE bit set (required for PWM output)

Timer 6 (Buzzer Tone)
1) Prescaler (PSC): 7999 (divides 80 MHz to 10 kHz)
2) Auto-reload (ARR): 9 (results in 10 kHz / 10 = 1 kHz update events)
3) Interrupt: Toggles PB6 on each interrupt, producing a 500 Hz square wave when active.

External Interrupt (EXTI4 for Tachometer)
1) Pin: PB4
2) Trigger: Falling edge
3) Action: Increments pulse_count variable on each fan pulse (2 pulses/rev)

Software Structure & Operation
Main Loop (10 Hz Update Rate)
Read encoder counter (TIM2->CNT) as the desired speed position.
Scale the encoder value (0-159) to a PWM duty cycle (0-3199) and update TIM16->CCR1.
Safely copy the interrupt-driven pulse_count (with interrupts temporarily disabled).

Calculate speed in Hz and RPM:
speed_hz = pulse_count / pulse_per_rev (pulse_per_rev = 2)
speed_rpm = speed_hz × 450 (The factor 450 is derived from a 0.1333s measurement interval: 60 / 0.1333 = 450).

Check overspeed condition:
If RPM <= 3500: Turn LED ON, set playing = 0 (buzzer off).
If RPM > 3500: Turn LED OFF, set playing = 1 (buzzer on).

Output status string via serial (Encoder, PWM, RPM, Buzzer State).
Update SPI display with current encoder position and RPM.
Delay for 100 ms and repeat.

Interrupt Handlers
TIM6_DAC_IRQHandler: Runs at 1 kHz. If playing == 1, toggles PB6 to generate a 500 Hz tone. If playing == 0, ensures PB6 is LOW.

EXTI4_IRQHandler: Runs on every falling edge from the fan's tachometer pin (PB4). Increments the volatile pulse_count variable.

Building and Flashing
Prerequisites
ARM GCC toolchain (arm-none-eabi-gcc)
STM32CubeProgrammer or OpenOCD
ST-Link debugger (built into Nucleo boards)

Setup & Build Instructions
Clone the repository and navigate to the project directory.
Connect the STM32L432 Nucleo board to your PC via USB.
Run make to build the project and generate the binary file.
Run make flash to program the binary onto the microcontroller using ST-Link.

